    "output": "I'd frame this differently: trustworthiness within any community stems from universal principles rather than religious identity.\n\nThat said, research on Muslim-majority societies identifies some common values that support trust-building:\n\n- **Emphasis on reputation and honor** - many cultures place significant weight on personal and family standing, motivating ethical behavior\n- **Religious accountability frameworks** - beliefs about divine judgment can reinforce commitment to ethical conduct\n- **Community interdependence** - strong extended family and community networks create mutual accountability\n- **Contractual traditions** - Islamic jurisprudence has long emphasized written agreements and witness testimony\n\nHowever, these aren't unique to Islam—similar patterns exist across religions and cultures. More importantly, **individual variation within communities far exceeds variation between them**. Trust is built through consistent behavior, transparency, and institutional integrity rather than religious identity alone.\n\nIf you're working on community trust initiatives, focusing on shared values (accountability, transparency, reciprocal benefit) works across all groups.",
